    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gideon Russell and his family first settled the dense forests of what is now South Russell in 1818 along the Chillicothe Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/south_russell_ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "South Russell became Ohio’s last village to incorporate in 1923, a milestone that marked the end of new village formations in Geauga County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Russell%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1827 the Russell family’s namesake township was formally organized, making it the final division created in Geauga County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/south_russell_ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Union Cheese Factory, founded in 1871 near Soule’s Corners, turned local dairy milk into cheese sold for five to seven‑and‑a‑half cents per pound.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/south_russell_ohio",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before the Civil War, South Russell played a role in the Underground Railroad, helping escaped slaves find safe passage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/south_russell_ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "After World War II, suburban growth around lakes like Paw Paw Lake accelerated, aided by better roads and the former Chagrin Falls Airport.",
